---
/**
 * How cable fault location works: port of design/mockups/fault-location.html (pages-b.css).
 * Graticule head with the four steps as the page index, fault types, the cable
 * cross-section, the four method steps with their diagrams (wide and narrow SVG variants,
 * switched in CSS at 600px), the van, and the quote band. Step 1 holds the interactive
 * TDR lab (src/scripts/method.ts).
 */
import { getImage, Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import QuoteBand from '../components/QuoteBand.astro';
import { absoluteUrl, breadcrumbs, organizationRef } from '../lib/structured-data';
import vanInterior from '../assets/img/van-interior.jpg';
import '../styles/pages-b.css';

const title = 'How cable fault location works | HV Discover';
const description =
  'How an underground medium-voltage cable fault is found: fault types, the cable, then four steps from TDR pre-location to route tracing.';
const articleImage = await getImage({ src: vanInterior, width: 1200, format: 'jpg' });

const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'How cable fault location works',
  description,
  inLanguage: 'en-GB',
  url: absoluteUrl('/fault-location'),
  mainEntityOfPage: absoluteUrl('/fault-location'),
  image: absoluteUrl(articleImage.src),
  about: { '@type': 'Thing', name: 'Fault location in underground medium-voltage power cables' },
  proficiencyLevel: 'Beginner',
  author: organizationRef(),
  publisher: organizationRef(),
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[article, breadcrumbs([{ name: 'Fault location', path: '/fault-location' }])]}
>
  <!-- HEAD: the method in one line, the four steps as the page index -->
  <section class="hero hero--page" aria-labelledby="page-title">
    <div class="wrap hero__inner">
      <h1 class="display" id="page-title">How cable fault location works</h1>
      <p class="hero__sub">A buried cable fails somewhere under a street. Finding it takes four measurements, each one narrowing the search: from the whole cable, to a distance in metres, to the spot where you dig.</p>

      <nav aria-label="The four steps on this page">
        <ol class="stations">
          <li class="station">
            <span class="station__node" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="station__num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
            <p class="h3 station__title"><span class="sr-only">Step 1: </span><a href="#step-1">Pre-locate</a></p>
            <p class="station__text">A low-voltage pulse and its echo give the distance to the fault.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="station">
            <span class="station__node" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="station__num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
            <p class="h3 station__title"><span class="sr-only">Step 2: </span><a href="#step-2">Condition</a></p>
            <p class="station__text">A high-voltage surge makes hidden faults break down and show.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="station">
            <span class="station__node" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="station__num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
            <p class="h3 station__title"><span class="sr-only">Step 3: </span><a href="#step-3">Pinpoint</a></p>
            <p class="station__text">The discharge is heard through the ground, loudest above the fault.</p>
          </li>
          <li class="station">
            <span class="station__node" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="station__num" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
            <p class="h3 station__title"><span class="sr-only">Step 4: </span><a href="#step-4">Trace route</a></p>
            <p class="station__text">The cable path puts the measured distance on the right street.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </nav>
      <p class="hero__note">Everything on this page is high-voltage work. Isolate, earth and test under your utility&rsquo;s switching and safety rules before connecting any instrument.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAULT TYPES -->
  <section class="section" id="fault-types" aria-labelledby="types-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2 id="types-title">What a cable fault is</h2>
        <p class="lead">A point where the insulation between the conductor and the earthed screen has failed, or where the conductor itself is broken. Its resistance decides how you find it.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="fault-legend" id="types-legend">In each sketch the top line is the conductor and the bottom line is the earthed metallic screen.</p>
      <ul class="fault-types" aria-describedby="types-legend">
        <li class="fault-type plate">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 240 96" role="img" aria-label="Sketch: the conductor is broken, the screen is intact.">
            <path class="dg-line-2" d="M12 32H108M132 32H228M108 25V39M132 25V39"/>
            <path class="dg-grey" d="M12 72H228"/>
            <circle class="dg-point" cx="120" cy="32" r="4.5"/>
          </svg>
          <h3>Open circuit</h3>
          <p>The conductor is broken. The pulse reflects back with the same polarity it was sent with.</p>
          <p class="fault-type__tdr is-yes">Low-voltage TDR alone: visible</p>
        </li>
        <li class="fault-type plate">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 240 96" role="img" aria-label="Sketch: a solid connection between the conductor and the screen.">
            <path class="dg-line-2" d="M12 32H228M120 32V72"/>
            <path class="dg-grey" d="M12 72H228"/>
            <circle class="dg-point" cx="120" cy="52" r="4.5"/>
          </svg>
          <h3>Short circuit</h3>
          <p>A low-resistance path from the conductor to the screen or another phase. The reflection comes back inverted.</p>
          <p class="fault-type__tdr is-yes">Low-voltage TDR alone: visible</p>
        </li>
        <li class="fault-type plate">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 240 96" role="img" aria-label="Sketch: a resistor between the conductor and the screen.">
            <path class="dg-line-2" d="M12 32H228"/>
            <path class="dg-line" d="M120 32V40L128 44L112 52L128 60L112 68L120 72"/>
            <path class="dg-grey" d="M12 72H228"/>
            <circle class="dg-point" cx="120" cy="56" r="4.5"/>
          </svg>
          <h3>High-resistance</h3>
          <p>A leakage path of hundreds of ohms or more. It barely changes the cable&rsquo;s impedance, so a low-voltage pulse passes it by.</p>
          <p class="fault-type__tdr">Low-voltage TDR alone: hidden, condition first</p>
        </li>
        <li class="fault-type plate">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 240 96" role="img" aria-label="Sketch: a spark gap between the conductor and the screen.">
            <path class="dg-line-2" d="M12 32H228M120 32V42M120 72V62"/>
            <path class="dg-fill" d="M113 41H127L120 49ZM113 63H127L120 55Z"/>
            <path class="dg-grey" d="M12 72H228"/>
            <circle class="dg-point" cx="120" cy="52" r="2.5"/>
          </svg>
          <h3>Intermittent</h3>
          <p>Holds at low voltage and flashes over only above a certain voltage, often only in service. Between flashovers it looks healthy.</p>
          <p class="fault-type__tdr">Low-voltage TDR alone: hidden, condition first</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="fault-note">Rule of thumb: power cables have a characteristic impedance of roughly 10 to 50&nbsp;&Omega;. A shunt fault well below that reflects strongly. One many times higher, from about a kilohm upwards, reflects too little to see, which is why many faults in medium-voltage cables need step 2 before they can be measured.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CABLE CROSS-SECTION -->
  <section class="section section--white" id="cable" aria-labelledby="cable-title">
    <div class="wrap xsec">
      <div class="xsec__text">
        <h2 id="cable-title">Inside a medium-voltage cable</h2>
        <p>A test pulse does not run along the copper alone. It travels between the conductor and the metallic screen, guided through the insulation. So the insulation sets its speed, and every measurement starts with knowing the cable.</p>
        <dl class="layers">
          <div><dt>Conductor</dt><dd>Stranded copper or aluminium. Carries the load current and the test pulse.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Conductor screen</dt><dd>Semiconductive layer that smooths the electric field at the strands.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>XLPE insulation</dt><dd>Holds the voltage. Its permittivity sets how fast the pulse travels.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Insulation screen</dt><dd>Semiconductive layer that keeps the field inside the insulation.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Metallic screen</dt><dd>Copper wires or tape, earthed. The return path for fault current and for the pulse.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Outer sheath</dt><dd>PE or PVC, often red. Keeps water out. A damaged sheath is a fault of its own.</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <div class="velocity">
          <p><b>Velocity of propagation.</b> In XLPE cables the pulse travels at about <span class="nowrap">160 m/&micro;s</span>, a little over half the speed of light. Reflectometers use half that figure, <b>v/2</b>, because the pulse goes out and back.</p>
          <p>A typical v/2 for XLPE is <b class="nowrap">80 m/&micro;s</b>; paper-insulated cables are usually a little slower. Confirm the value on a healthy phase or a known length before you trust a distance.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="xsec__fig plate">
        <svg class="diagram dg-wide" viewBox="0 0 520 340" role="img" aria-labelledby="xs-title xs-desc">
          <title id="xs-title">Cross-section of a single-core XLPE medium-voltage cable</title>
          <desc id="xs-desc">From the centre outwards: a stranded copper conductor, a thin black conductor screen, a thick milky-white XLPE insulation, a black insulation screen, a ring of copper screen wires on a bedding tape, and a red outer sheath.</desc>
