                </svg>
              </div>
              <figcaption class="figure-caption">The reference shows only the cable end: the high-resistance fault is invisible to it. During the arc the fault behaves like a short and reflects inverted.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <dl class="method-facts">
                <div><dt>What you measure</dt><dd>The distance at which the arc trace departs from the reference trace.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Typical pitfall</dt><dd>Surging higher than the fault needs. Raise the voltage in steps, and stay within the test voltage allowed for the cable.</dd></div>
                <div><dt>Instruments</dt><dd>
                  <ul class="station__products">
                    <li><a href="/products/pg-10-surge-generator"><span class="station__model">PG-10</span> <span class="station__pname">Surge generator, 10 kV</span></a></li>
                    <li><a href="/products/pg-32-surge-generator"><span class="station__model">PG-32</span> <span class="station__pname">Surge generator, 30 kV</span></a></li>
                    <li><a href="/products/arm-filter"><span class="station__model">ARM Filter</span> <span class="station__pname">with the TDR-901, for arc reflection</span></a></li>
                  </ul>
                </dd></div>
              </dl>
          </div>
        </li>

        <!-- STEP 3 -->
        <li class="method-step" id="step-3" aria-labelledby="step-3-title">
          <span class="method-step__node" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <div class="method-step__head">
            <span class="method-step__num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
            <h2 id="step-3-title"><span class="sr-only">Step 3: </span>Pinpoint: find the spot to dig</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="method-step__grid">
            <div class="method-step__text">
                <p>A pre-located distance still carries the uncertainty of v/2 and of the route, often several metres. Digging needs the exact spot.</p>
                <p>With the surge generator firing at a steady rate, every discharge makes a sharp thump at the fault, and the sound travels up through the soil. Walk the traced route around the pre-located distance with a ground microphone. The thump is loudest directly above the fault and fades on either side.</p>
                <p>Compare loudness, not just presence: on hard surfaces and along ducts the sound can carry several metres.</p>
              </div>
            <figure class="method-step__fig">
              <div class="plate">
                <svg class="diagram dg-wide" viewBox="0 0 520 320" role="img" aria-labelledby="ac-title ac-desc">
                  <title id="ac-title">Acoustic pinpointing</title>
